  1	export function createLatestQueryRunner(query, view) {
  2	  let currentRequest = 0;
  3	  return filters => {
  4	    const request = ++currentRequest;
  5	    view.start();
  6	    query({ ...filters })
  7	      .then(result => {
  8	        if (request === currentRequest) view.succeed(result);
  9	      })
 10	      .catch(error => {
 11	        if (request === currentRequest) view.fail(error);
 12	      })
 13	      .finally(() => {
 14	        if (request === currentRequest) view.finish();
 15	      });
 16	  };
 17	}
  1	# Product and order filtering design
  2	
  3	## Outcome and contracts
  4	
  5	The application has two URL-addressable pages. Products expose keyword, category,
  6	and publication-status filters as an editable draft that is published only by
  7	Apply. Orders expose keyword, date range, and order-status filters; every valid
  8	edit is published immediately. Reset publishes each page's documented defaults.
  9	Unknown query parameters are preserved, and `popstate` rebuilds both controls and
 10	results from the URL.
 11	
 12	The URL stores only applied values (`p_q`, `p_category`, `p_status`, `o_q`,
 13	`o_from`, `o_to`, `o_status`). Parsers accept only known enum values and valid ISO
 14	calendar dates, falling back per field to page defaults. Serializers omit default
 15	values so links remain concise. Product defaults are empty keyword, all categories,
 16	and published status. Order defaults are empty keyword, all statuses, and the 30
 17	calendar days ending on `today` from `data.mjs`.
 18	
 19	Order date edits remain in the controls while either value is incomplete or the
 20	range is reversed. The form shows an associated error and retains the previously
 21	applied URL and results. Once the range is valid, the whole draft is published.
 22	This distinction keeps editable state separate from navigable state.
 23	
 24	Every query has a monotonically increasing identity. Only the current identity may
 25	publish success, error, or completion feedback; superseded responses are ignored.
 26	The previous result stays visible while a query is pending or fails. A current
 27	failure is presented as an alert, while a current success replaces rows, count,
 28	and any prior query error together.
 29	
 30	## Architecture
 31	
 32	```mermaid
 33	flowchart TD
 34	  Browser[Browser URL and history] --> App[app.mjs page composer]
 35	  UI[ui.mjs native controls] --> App
 36	  App --> Codec[filter-state.mjs parse and serialize]
 37	  Codec --> Browser
 38	  App --> Runner[latest-query.mjs query runner]
 39	  Runner --> Query[query.mjs data access]
 40	  Query --> Data[data.mjs fixtures]
 41	  Runner --> View[status, alert, and Results list]
 42	```
 43	
 44	`ui.mjs` continues to own reusable labelled native controls. `filter-state.mjs`
 45	owns pure filter defaults, URL codecs, and date-range validation. `app.mjs` owns
 46	page semantics, drafts, URL transitions, history restoration, and result view.
 47	`latest-query.mjs` owns request identity and publication order, while `query.mjs`
 48	remains the asynchronous data boundary. Both pages share
 49	the navigation and result shell, while their distinct application rules remain in
 50	separate setup functions.
 51	
 52	## Data flow
 53	
 54	```mermaid
 55	sequenceDiagram
 56	  actor User
 57	  participant Control
 58	  participant Page as Page owner
 59	  participant URL as history/URL
 60	  participant Query
 61	  participant Results
 62	  User->>Control: edit or submit
 63	  Control->>Page: page-specific draft
 64	  alt valid publish trigger
 65	    Page->>URL: push applied filter snapshot
 66	    Page->>Query: run(snapshot, request id)
 67	    Query-->>Page: rows or error
 68	    Page->>Results: publish only if id is current
 69	  else invalid order dates
 70	    Page->>Control: associated validation error
 71	    Note over URL,Results: applied state remains unchanged
 72	  end
 73	  User->>URL: back / forward / reload
 74	  URL->>Page: parse applied state
 75	  Page->>Control: restore draft
 76	  Page->>Query: run(restored snapshot)
 77	```
