 79	## Implementation sequence and acceptance
 80	
 81	1. Add URL/date helpers in `filter-state.mjs`: parse canonical page filters, serialize by
 82	   replacing only owned keys, and compute the inclusive 30-day order default.
 83	   Acceptance: unrelated parameters survive; unknown enums/dates become defaults;
 84	   history and reload recreate controls and queries.
 85	2. Add a shared result view and latest-result runner. The runner receives an
 86	   immutable filter snapshot and query function, and owns request identity and
 87	   current feedback. Acceptance: slow earlier success/error/finalization cannot
 88	   replace current rows or feedback; visible counts equal returned rows.
 89	3. Compose the product page with native controls and form submission. Draft edits
 90	   have no external effect; Apply pushes the complete draft and queries; Reset
 91	   publishes product defaults. Acceptance: Enter follows native submit behavior,
 92	   category/status rules stay product-specific, and back/forward restores drafts.
 93	4. Compose the order page with native controls and input/change publication.
 94	   Validate both dates as a single range before URL/query effects; Reset publishes
 95	   the documented range. Acceptance: invalid drafts remain editable with an alert
 96	   associated to both date inputs, while valid edits immediately update results.
 97	5. Make the form layout intrinsic and wrapping, with controls allowed to shrink to
 98	   the container. Acceptance: native keyboard behavior remains intact and the page
 99	   has no horizontal overflow at 360 px with 200% text and long labels.
100	6. Run the browser acceptance suite and inspect changed callers. A realistic next
101	   change—adding a new enum option—should require updating only its options/default
102	   and the relevant page codec/control, without changing request-ordering policy.
